<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import SignIn from './SignIn.abide'
import Badge from './Badge.abide'
import Density from './Density.abide'
import signInSource from './SignIn.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import headerSource from './Header.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import badgeSource from './Badge.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import densitySource from './Density.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="One seal, read everywhere">
        <Example
            id="principal-session"
            title="`principal.set` — one seal, read everywhere"
            summary="A sign-in on a CRM, and the header above it. Sign in, then sign out, and watch a component the form never names."
            files={{
                'SignIn.abide': signInSource,
                'Header.abide': headerSource,
            }}
        >
            <SignIn />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 ambient read, 0 wires</em> — the arm holds the seal and the resolved claims as two
        variables and a reader list, because the header the form never names has to be told.</p>

        <p><code>principal.set(claims)</code> authenticates this caller: the claims are sealed with
        <code>ABIDE_PRINCIPAL_SECRET</code> into an <code>HttpOnly</code> cookie living
        <code>ABIDE_PRINCIPAL_TTL</code>, and <code>principal.clear()</code> signs them out. What
        you put in is yours — an id, a tenant, a role — and abide's part is that it comes back
        unforged.</p>

        <p>The seal is an ordinary JWT, signed HS256 (26.11): the claims are its payload's own
        members beside <code>exp</code> and <code>iat</code>, which are abide's. So another service
        holding the same secret verifies the cookie with any JWT library, and a token that names
        another algorithm is no seal at all.</p>

        <p>Both run in a handler, because the cookie is written on the answer. The answer also tells
        the browser the principal moved, and the browser loads the document again under the new
        cookie — so the header above the form reads the new name when <code>signIn</code> returns,
        with nothing passed to it. The tab that signed in tells the browser's other tabs over a
        <code>BroadcastChannel</code>, and each reloads from the cookie they share (26.24), so
        signing out in one signs out the page in all of them.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The five reads">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>principal.authenticated</code></td><td>whether this caller presented something the server accepted</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>principal.resolved</code></td><td>what <code>onPrincipal</code> returned, merged over the baseline</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>principal.expiresAt</code></td><td>when the seal lapses</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>principal.error</code></td><td>the app's resolver having failed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>principal.caller</code></td><td><strong>which browser</strong>, as against who they are</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Each is a <code>Reactive</code>, so reading one in a template subscribes and a sign-out
        moves every place that reads it. Each is request-local on a server, so a handler reads the
        principal of the request that reached it.</p>

        <p><code>principal.caller</code> is the one worth pausing on. It identifies a
        <strong>browser</strong>, not a person, and it is there whether or not anybody has signed in
        — which is what a rate limiter, a per-device preference and a cache key want. Reaching for
        it as an identity is the mistake it is named to prevent.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`onPrincipal` turns claims into your app's user">
        <Example
            id="principal-resolve"
            title="`onPrincipal(fn)` — an id in the cookie, a user on the page"
            summary="A CRM that seals nothing but an account id. Sign in as either account and the name and role arrive anyway, looked up on the server."
            files={{
                'Badge.abide': badgeSource,
            }}
        >
            <Badge />
        </Example>

        <p>What it returns is merged under the baseline and read as <code>principal.resolved</code>.
        So the cookie stays small — an id — and the page still reads a name, because the resolution
        happens on the server where the record is. The baseline's <code>authenticated</code>,
        <code>expiresAt</code> and <code>error</code> win a collision, so a resolver cannot sign a
        caller in by returning <code>authenticated: true</code>.</p>

        <p>A resolver that fails fills <code>principal.error</code> rather than throwing out of
        every read. A caller whose seal is valid and whose <em>record</em> could not be loaded is a
        distinguishable state, and it is one an app usually wants to render rather than
        escalate.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/run-code-at-start-and-stop')}>Lifecycle</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`Principal` is the wire document">
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type Principal = { authenticated, expiresAt?, error?, …claims }`} />

        <p><code>authenticated</code>, <code>expiresAt</code> and <code>error</code> are abide's
        three fields, and everything else in the document is yours. It is what the browser is
        served, which is what makes the same reads work there — a page rendered on the server and
        hydrated in a browser reads <code>principal.resolved.name</code> in both, and neither
        branches on where it is.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`cookies` is the seam underneath">
        <Example
            id="cookies-density"
            title="`cookies()` — one jar, read on both sides"
            summary="The contact list's density, kept in a cookie. Pick one and reload the page — the server paints the one you picked."
            files={{
                'Density.abide': densitySource,
            }}
        >
            <Density />
        </Example>

        <p><code>cookies()</code> reads and writes the request's cookies on a server and the
        document's in a browser — one jar, the shape of Bun's <code>CookieMap</code>, under one
        name. A read by name tracks that name (22.4), so the tab follows the write, and the server
        render reads the same cookie off the request, so a reload paints the density you picked
        rather than flashing the default first.</p>

        <p>The principal cookie is one it manages. Reaching for it directly is for the cookies that
        are yours — a theme, a consent flag, a last-viewed id.</p>

        <p>The principal cookie is <code>HttpOnly</code>, so script cannot read it. That is the
        point: the claims reach the page through the <code>Principal</code> document the server
        rendered, and the <em>seal</em> never leaves the header.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/configure-the-app')}>Config</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> — deciding what
            a caller may do</li>
            <li><a href={url('/app/configure-the-app')}>Config</a> — where the secret and the TTL
            are read</li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/ambient-values')}>Ambient values</a> —
            <code>principal</code> beside the rest</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
